{
  "step_1": "The decision reason can be applied to the decision problem. The reason focuses on pairwise differences between outcomes of the two lotteries, considering all possible outcome pairs (each outcome of Lottery A paired with each outcome of Lottery B), and sums the differences. The relevant information includes the distinct outcomes of each lottery: Lottery A has outcomes of -4000, -2000, and 0 (with respective probabilities), and Lottery B has outcomes of -6000 and 0. The pairwise differences are computed as follows: (-4000) - (-6000) = +2000; (-4000) - 0 = -4000; (-2000) - (-6000) = +4000; (-2000) - 0 = -2000; 0 - (-6000) = +6000; 0 - 0 = 0. The sum of all pairwise differences is 2000 - 4000 + 4000 - 2000 + 6000 + 0 = 6000. Since the sum is positive, the reason would favor Lottery A (as the differences are calculated as A - B). Thus, the rule can be applied, and it prefers Lottery A based on a more favorable sum of outcome differences.",
